I downloaded the neo demo on its last  day and I've made a mistake this game is  amazing and I have less than 24 hours to  play it so I opened my notepad I start  taking a few notes on the game and then  I discard that idea because I do not  intend on putting this controller down I  have this legitimate hunger for the  combat just to dive in and kill enemies  and push a little bit further than I got  last time and that's a damn good sign so  here's what I learned and here's what I  was impressed with from my short time  playing in this game stamina is called  key and it's key that you manage your  key and the key of your opponent with  this new information you have the power  to see how far your enemy can exert  themselves when they're about to  overextend and when the right moment is  to overwhelm them and punish them right  off the bat this adds a huge depth to  the combat system but most importantly  perhaps stamina vision affords you this  reassuring feeling that the opponent's  you're facing are operating on the same  rules that you are as a player the game  itself isn't trying to kill you the AI  within the game are and I think that's a  humanizing rule of design that more  games should follow especially games  like this that go even beyond Dark Souls  in terms of how hard they are an  additional mechanic to make stamina more  interactive is the key pulse  if you press r1 a moment after expending  your key then you'll perform this pulse  which immediately regenerates the key  you just expended the amount is  signified by this fast-moving blue bar  and doing this pulse properly forces  this flow upon combat which if followed  appropriately gives you a huge key  advantage over your opponents and it  just allows you to attack like an  absolute demon provided you can keep up  the appropriate pacing also this pulse  can cleanse the aura given off by  enemies from the yokai  it's this black fog that if you step in  it stops you regenerating your key so  performing the pulse to cleanse this  felt to me like a forceful way of  teaching me that the key pulse habit is  probably one I should get into  unfortunately I didn't during my time in  the demo and I have a huge feeling I  made the game much much harder for  myself because I didn't start doing this  but anyway all of this makes neo the  first souls like game that I've played  that puts stamina at the very core of  the game where it rightfully belongs  this fits into this combat system that  rewards awareness and it's not just  awareness of stamina it's also awareness  of the attacks of enemies of course and  in these kinds of games enemy attacks  should have two parts there's the  forecast of the attack and then there's  the attack itself there's this raising  or lowering of the weapon than the swing  allowing you to predict and react the  animation is fantastic for the most part  except on some of the larger monsters  for example this one boss had this spin  attack that I never really felt like I  deserved getting hit by and they need to  be careful to get this right because I  think it's the most important thing  about the combat one thing that took a  lot of getting used to is the sheer  variety of move sets that are available  to both you and your opponents in neo  there is a selection of weapon classes  like spear hammer katana and jewel  katana and while every weapon within  that class appears to share the same  moveset every class of weapon can be  held in one of three stances so high  stance gives you powerful but slower  attacks low stance gives you fast but  weaker attacks and mid stance is  predictably the balance between the two  and in each stance you still have access  to light and heavy attacks and these  attacks are different depending on what  stance you're in so basically you can  swap a TAC style on the fly and that  feels fantastic it's quite like  blood-borne in that  regarde just instead of having two  weapon states every weapon has three  weapon states and it means you can  proactively adapt to combat in a way  that you never really do in souls games  giving you access to thrust attacks  overhead attacks fast attacks slow  attacks all regardless of the weapon  you're using and you might already be  thinking about this but the obvious risk  here is that because you can cover the  weaknesses of every single weapon by  changing stances that weapons might  struggle to feel unique because what's  the point in using a great hammer if I  can just put it in a light stance to  attack quickly or what's the point of  using dual katanas if I can put it in a  slow stance to make them more powerful  so yeah this does affect the  individuality of weapon classes and sure  you could argue that yes the low stance  of the duel katanas is still faster than  the low stance of the great hammer but  there's so much coverage here and this  approach does mean that weapons lose  some individuality but do I want it  changed nope like I said it feels  fantastic to have complete control of  your weapon and the only thing holding  you back in combat is really your  awareness and your knowledge of how well  your stance will fit the situation I  think that build diversity is going to  struggle a bit in the long run because  of this but there is so much depth to  the combat that I don't see this being a  big issue and for all the individuality  weapons lose from stances they do gain  some character back with the option to  learn weapons specific abilities you can  invest talent points into new abilities  which are specific to each weapon class  like the powerful spin attack of the  great hammer or the wily trip attack of  the spear and also you can find the same  type of weapon with different rarities  and attributes and that helps make your  weapon feel a bit more unique I'd also  like to praise the level design  particularly for the way that these felt  like real coastlines real villages real  forests and elect the way they blended  environments together and  all the while they were hiding a bunch  of estes shards physical shortcuts and  most notably ambushes because of these i  was progressing slowly peeking around  corners and just listening for that  audio cue that signals you're in trouble  there was no real cue for this happening  however and speaking of falling  I like how knocking enemies off edges  was actually encouraged by the level  design and the combat because if you  strike an enemy towards the edge then  they'll clip into this falling animation  that's incredibly satisfying to pull off  and this ability to jostle for space and  knock enemies off ledges it's supported  by the combat much more than insoles  because if you hit an enemy in this game  or if you take a hit there is a lot more  back step and movement during attacks  that you need to take into account now  it is possible to comfortably run past  enemies but this demo had a lot of  decent choke points that's something  that Dark Souls never does well you can  always sprint past stuff and then  initiate the invincibility frames when  you're opening a door but no in this  game if you're climbing a ladder and you  get hit once you fall off and good luck  killing a mini-boss that's a required  kill when you're followed by all the  enemies that you've accrued from running  for 5 minutes straight I was always  punished for fighting in unfamiliar  territory and that's a good thing  you're also rewarded for fighting some  enemies as the youkai monsters don't  respawn if you've killed them once which  allows them to serve as a sort of soft  checkpoint system and I probably  shouldn't talk about the game too much  longer because I didn't get enough time  with neo to talk confidently about it  but man considering how much fun I had  with it and how hyped I am for it now I  just couldn't resist making a video but  rest assured when this game does come  out on February 19th 2017 I will try to  make the best damn neo video you've ever  seen but for now I'm going to direct you  over to three channels there's Tara  mantises channel he has a great video on  neo  multiplayer and I'm going to direct you  to a smaller channel I found called  hustle habit for a chilled-out  discussion about the depth in the game  and also silvermont has this fantastic  video talking about how well the  developer listened to feedback since  their previous demo which you really do  have to give the dev props for from his  video I got the impression that they  literally took his advice on some topics  and they've even reached out to me to  hear what scant feedback I have and  that's super humbling and very promising  we've covered a few souls like games at  this point some of them take the souls  formula and they apply it cleverly to an  entirely different 2d perspective some  take a few elements they twist them and  they build them up and turn the game  into something else and sometimes  something better these little risks and  show how the formula of souls can be  improved and they're the way these games  can continue to feel fresh and I'm  expecting a lot of great things from neo  when I pick it up for ps4 so thanks for  watching guys and I'll see you in the  next one
 

